## Bundled runtime libraries

AirPlayEngine's C target links against these libraries at build/run time
via Homebrew (see AirPlayEngine/Package.swift). In a release/distribution
build (`scripts/make-app.sh` run with `AUDIOUTER_BUNDLE_DYLIBS=1`), these
libraries are copied into `Contents/Frameworks/` inside the shipped
`.app` so it can run without a Homebrew install present. A plain local
dev build (the flag unset) still links them from the developer's own
Homebrew install and does not bundle anything.

Because libgcrypt, libgpg-error, and libplist are
LGPL-2.1-or-later, and ffmpeg may be LGPL-2.1-or-later or
GPL-2.0-or-later depending on build configuration, bundling these
libraries into the app does trigger LGPL redistribution obligations
that did not apply when they were only dynamically loaded from a
user-managed Homebrew install.

The compliance mechanism used: each library ships as its own separate,
individually replaceable `.dylib` file inside `Contents/Frameworks/` —
never statically merged into AirPlayEngine's or Audiouter's own binary.
This is intended to satisfy LGPL §6's requirement that a user be able to
relink or substitute a compatible version of the library. This is not a
legal guarantee; it is the mechanism this project relies on for
compliance.

Separately, the whole app is already GPL-2.0-or-later (per the GPL
sender cluster above), so a GPL-built ffmpeg is license-compatible
either way — bundling ffmpeg does not introduce any GPL/LGPL conflict.

Libraries, listed for attribution and so contributors know what to
`brew install` for a dev build:

- libevent (BSD-3-Clause)
- libsodium (ISC)
- libgcrypt, libgpg-error (LGPL-2.1-or-later)
- libplist (LGPL-2.1-or-later)
- ffmpeg (LGPL-2.1-or-later / GPL-2.0-or-later depending on build
  configuration) — libavcodec, libavutil, libswresample

Note: the exact set of files that land in `Contents/Frameworks/` —
especially ffmpeg's transitive dependencies (libraries such as x264,
aom, dav1d, or others depending on how ffmpeg was built) — is
determined by an `otool -L` dependency walk performed at build time by
`scripts/make-app.sh`, and can vary with the Homebrew version used to
build. This NOTICE describes the top-level libraries and the bundling
mechanism; the authoritative exact file list for any given build is
whatever is actually present in that build's `Contents/Frameworks/`,
not a list frozen here.
